Extended notes for therock on Slackware
======================================

This file contains additional build and runtime notes that do not
fit in the main README. Read this if your build fails, your GPU
compute is denied, or you are packaging/hosting binaries.


Build environment notes
-----------------------

The TheRock tag tarball (TheRock-$VERSION.tar.gz) is only the
bootstrap; the build system fetches the actual component sources
itself. Full builds take hours to days depending on hardware.
Plan disk accordingly: the source cache and build tree together
can consume tens of gigabytes.

The virtual environment used for TheRock's Python tooling is
created inside the persistent source cache and never touches the
system Python. Requirements are installed from the tag's own
requirements.txt so version pins always match the tree being
built.

Rebuilding the same version is safe: upstream fetching resets
component trees to pristine state. Do NOT hand-edit sources in
the cache expecting edits to survive a re-run or version bump --
they will not. Carry patches as a separate step after fetch.


Device permissions and verification
-----------------------------------

On stock Slackware-current both /dev/kfd and /dev/dri/renderD*
are created world read/write (0666):

  crw-rw-rw- 1 root video 226, 128 ... /dev/dri/renderD128
  crw-rw-rw- 1 root video  508,   0 ... /dev/kfd

No group membership is required for GPU compute access. Expect
one renderD node per display adapter -- CPUs with integrated
graphics (AMD 7000/9000 series, most modern Intel) add a node.

If your system is hardened and these nodes are 0660:

  # usermod -aG video yourusername

then log out and back in. Slackware has no 'render' group; for a
dedicated compute group instead of video:

  # groupadd -r render
  # echo 'KERNEL=="kfd", GROUP="render", MODE="0660"' \
      > /etc/udev/rules.d/70-kfd.rules
  # udevadm trigger --name-match=kfd
  # usermod -aG render yourusername

Remember: group changes do not affect running sessions. Log out
and back in before retesting.


Troubleshooting
---------------

* rocminfo fails mentioning 'kfd':
  Check device permissions first (above). This is the most common
  first-contact failure and almost always permissions.

* Integrated GPUs (AMD Ryzen 7000/9000 iGPUs) appear in rocminfo:
  These are enumerated but not ROCm-supported for compute. This is
  normal and harmless.

* Workloads run on the wrong GPU:
  Device enumeration order is not guaranteed. Restrict with
  HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 or ROCR_VISIBLE_DEVICES set to the GPU
  UUID.


